From: Boccolarg <uranusthebest@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] KVM: arm64: nVHE: flush all TLBs when freeing a VM's stage 2

kvm_free_stage2_pgd() frees a VM's stage-2 page tables without any TLB
invalidation: the dead VM's VMID is not reused before the next VMID
rollover, whose flush_context() invalidates the TLBs of all VMIDs on all
CPUs (__kvm_flush_vm_context, TLBI ALLE1IS). For correctness that is
enough; for latency it is not. In nVHE mode, a CPU that ran a guest with
its stage-1 MMU on makes every broadcast TLB invalidation issued by the
host slower, on every CPU, until the rollover or a reboot: on the Kria
KV260 (Cortex-A53, no VHE), with stress-ng --tlb-shootdown on CPUs 0-2,
the cyclictest maximum on the isolated CPU 3 goes from 53-72 us to
83-164 us (also seen on Cortex-A55/A76 with kvm-arm.mode=nvhe, and on
6.12 and 7.1 kernels).

Invalidating the dead VM's VMID does not remove it, nor does invalidating
VMID 0 or the host's stage 1; only TLBI ALLE1IS does, as the rollover
proves. So do the rollover's invalidation when the stage 2 of a VM that
has run is freed, in nVHE mode. It costs one TLB refill on every CPU per
VM teardown.

This is the 6.18 form of kria-kvm's kvm_stage2_free_flush patch: the
RFC version sent to kvmarm (runphi_testing/kvm-nvhe-stale-tlb/draft),
which leaves VHE alone (it has no such effect there). Measurements:
runphi_testing/runphi-kvm-rt-kria and runphi-kvm-rt-opi5.

---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c b/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c
index 403a63548..491f88165 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.c
@@ -1070,6 +1070,17 @@ void kvm_free_stage2_pgd(struct kvm_s2_mmu *mmu)
 	write_lock(&kvm->mmu_lock);
 	pgt = mmu->pgt;
 	if (pgt) {
+		/*
+		 * The VM's VMID is not reused before the next rollover, which
+		 * invalidates all VMIDs, so its TLB entries can stay until then.
+		 * In nVHE mode, however, a CPU that ran a guest with its stage-1
+		 * MMU on makes the host's broadcast TLB invalidations more
+		 * expensive for all CPUs, and invalidating the VM's VMID does not
+		 * stop it: do the rollover's invalidation now. A VM that never
+		 * ran has no VMID (0).
+		 */
+		if (!has_vhe() && atomic64_read(&mmu->vmid.id))
+			kvm_call_hyp(__kvm_flush_vm_context);
 		mmu->pgd_phys = 0;
 		mmu->pgt = NULL;
 		free_percpu(mmu->last_vcpu_ran);
